Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  John Locke the 17th century English  philosopher argued that private property  arose when people mix their labor with  something not yet owned by anyone else  so if in the state of nature I clear  land in the wilderness and grow crops  there i acquire a right to the land and  the crops it would be wrong for someone  else to take them away without my  consent let's see it Locke's idea helps  us think about a controversy that arises  each winter in Boston after snowstorms  in Boston many people park their cars on  the street near their homes it takes a  long time to clear a parking place from  several feet of snow imagine your  frustration  if after spending hours digging out your  car you drive to the grocery store and  return to find another car parked in the  spot you dug out to prevent this people  who labor to clear snow from parking  spots install markers such as traffic  cones or lawn chairs to lay claim to  them at least for a few days should this  be permitted or should anyone be free to  use a public parking spot even if  someone else has labored long and hard  to clear it  [Music]

